# Hosted delivery mode (bd polyglot-pdf-92qe.2): `npm run build:hosted` loads
# this file (vite --mode hosted). The build talks to the backend's token-gated
# /api/editor routes instead of a local form-kits folder.
#
# VITE_API_BASE_URL: the Cloud Run backend URL. The hosted editor calls it
# DIRECTLY (not via the Firebase Hosting /api rewrite -- that rewrite has a 60s
# request cap that would kill minutes-long extraction runs). For a local
# full-stack test, point it at the local backend instead:
#   VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000
VITE_DELIVERY_MODE=hosted
VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://polyglot-backend-won24rgefq-uc.a.run.app

# Optional: bake an access code into the build (the original single-contractor
# plan). Normally LEAVE THIS UNSET -- the app then prompts for the code at first
# load and remembers it per browser, which is what lets one build serve both
# access tiers (editor vs author). NEVER write a real code into this file (it is
# COMMITTED); and know that ANY baked value -- from this file OR the shell env --
# is compiled in PLAINTEXT into the served JS bundle (standard Vite VITE_*
# behavior): anyone who can open the site can read it. That is the accepted
# tradeoff of the original one-trusted-contractor plan; with the access-code
# prompt it is simply unnecessary -- prefer the prompt.
#   (shell-env form, keeps it out of git but NOT out of the bundle:
#    VITE_EDITOR_TOKEN=... npm run build:hosted)
# VITE_EDITOR_TOKEN=
